The source is an ADS-B aircraft-tracking timeseries: each row is one timed state report for one airframe. Walking the columns:

Entity 1 — Aircraft. The icao24 column holds the ICAO 24-bit address, a fixed per-airframe identifier that repeats for the same aircraft across rows, so it is the subject. The ontology class Aircraft (subclass of Vehicle → Equipment → Materiel) fits; serial number (inherited from Materiel) takes icao24 as the airframe's registry-style identifier. callsign ('VIPER11') is a per-flight alternate name, mapped to alternate name on Battlespace Concept. squawk is a second identifier scheme: the schema actually shows multiple identifier schemes for the same entity (icao24 serial, callsign, squawk), which is exactly the structure Alternate Identifier models, so squawk becomes its own entity linked via has alternate identifier, with alternate identifier value from the squawk column and a constant standard code "squawk". Vehicle declares has observation, and each row is one such observation, so Aircraft links to per-row Observations.

Entity 2 — Observation. Keyed by time plus icao24 (both always populated), typed bsm:Observation, with observation date time from the time column. Because lat, lon, velocity, heading, and the altitudes vary per record, they belong to the observation, not the aircraft. Observation (a Battlespace Concept) inherits has geographic location, so the position becomes a Location → has geometry → Absolute Point chain. The unattached object properties has velocity, has orientation, has altitude attach naturally to the Observation.

Entity choices below the Observation: Velocity carries speed in m/s from the velocity column (values like 231.4 are plausible m/s). For heading there are two near-synonyms: absolute bearing angle on Velocity versus heading angle on Orientation. The qualifier decides: the column is literally named "heading", so heading angle on Orientation wins; the bearing alternative is noted as the ambiguity. Two altitude columns (baroaltitude, geoaltitude) both fit altitude in meters, so they become two distinct Altitude entities distinguished by their subject templates. lat/lon (54.401, 18.71, decimal degrees) match Absolute Point's WGS84 latitude/longitude properties.

Unmapped: vertrate is a vertical speed (m/s), not an angle — inclination angle would be a units mismatch — and onground is a boolean with no corresponding property (operating mode code expects a coded value, is active means something else).

Every iri-typed value reuses its target entity's subject_transformation string character-for-character (obs_{icao24}_{time}, loc_{icao24}_{time}, vel_{icao24}_{time}, ori_{icao24}_{time}, altbaro_{icao24}_{time}, altgeo_{icao24}_{time}, point_{icao24}_{time}, altid_{icao24}_{squawk}), all placeholders name real schema columns, optional transformation keys are omitted where raw values are used, and all class/property URIs appear verbatim in the ontology.

